---
import metrics from "../data/guardrail-metrics.json";
import latency from "../data/guardrail-latency.json";
import Stamp from "./Stamp.astro";
import { guardrailPlain } from "../content/copy";
import { guardrailDetails, guardrailRepo } from "../content/links";
import { guardrailNotes } from "../content/measured";
import { guardrailDiagram } from "../lib/diagram";
import { stemChart } from "../lib/chart";
import { formatInt, statusKind } from "../lib/format";

const medians = metrics.median_ns_per_op;
const byPath = (path: string) => {
  const row = medians.find((item) => item.path === path);
  if (!row) throw new Error(`Missing median: ${path}`);
  return row;
};

const allow = byPath("check: allow ($500.00)");
const needs = byPath("check: needs_approval ($500.01)");
const sign = byPath("approval sign + verify (no network)");
const proxyAllow = byPath("proxy allow, localhost upstream");
const roundTrip = byPath("proxy approval round trip, localhost webhook");
const redaction = byPath("PII redaction, 572-byte prompt");
const append = byPath("audit append with fsync");
const verify = byPath("audit verify 10000 entries");
const approvalDiff = roundTrip.ns_op - proxyAllow.ns_op;

const checkChart = stemChart([
  {
    label: allow.path,
    lines: ["allow", "$500.00"],
    value: allow.ns_op,
    display: formatInt(allow.ns_op),
    accent: true,
  },
  {
    label: needs.path,
    lines: ["needs approval", "$500.01"],
    value: needs.ns_op,
    display: formatInt(needs.ns_op),
  },
]);

const midChart = stemChart([
  {
    label: proxyAllow.path,
    lines: ["localhost allow"],
    value: proxyAllow.ns_op,
    display: formatInt(proxyAllow.ns_op),
  },
  {
    label: redaction.path,
    lines: ["redaction"],
    value: redaction.ns_op,
    display: formatInt(redaction.ns_op),
  },
  {
    label: roundTrip.path,
    lines: ["round trip"],
    value: roundTrip.ns_op,
    display: formatInt(roundTrip.ns_op),
  },
  {
    label: append.path,
    lines: ["fsync append"],
    value: append.ns_op,
    display: formatInt(append.ns_op),
    accent: true,
  },
]);

const diagram = guardrailDiagram();
const [demoA, demoB] = metrics.demo_runtime_seconds.runs;
if (demoA === undefined || demoB === undefined) throw new Error("Missing demo runs");

const milestones = metrics.tests_per_milestone.map((row) => {
  const detail = guardrailDetails[row.milestone];
  if (!detail) throw new Error(`Missing milestone copy: ${row.milestone}`);
  const prNumber = detail.pr.match(/\/pull\/(\d+)/)?.[1];
  if (!prNumber || row.test_cases === undefined || row.test_functions === undefined) {
    throw new Error(`Incomplete milestone: ${row.milestone}`);
  }
  return { ...row, ...detail, prNumber, kind: statusKind(row.status) };
});

const latencyExcerpt = latency
  .filter((row) => row.milestone === 1 || row.path.startsWith("audit"))
  .map((row) => `${row.path},${row.median_ns_op},${row.milestone}`)
  .join("\n");

const env = metrics.environment;
const corpus = metrics.pii_corpus;
---

<p class="plain" id="guardrail">{guardrailPlain[0]}</p>
<p class="plain">{guardrailPlain[1]}</p>
<h3 class="project-title">Deterministic guardrail and audit proxy</h3>
<p>
  <a href={guardrailRepo}>guardrail-proxy</a>
</p>
<p>
  I wrote a Go proxy that sits between an LLM agent and financial APIs. It checks every tool call
  against a strict schema and a deterministic rule before anything reaches the real API. The code
  uses the Go 1.22 standard library and no third-party dependencies.
</p>
<p>
  The schema rejects unknown tools and fields, missing or duplicate fields, and wrong types before
  any rule runs. Money is integer cents parsed straight from the JSON text. Floats are rejected, so
  $500.00 and $500.01 stay distinct. Transfers over $500 need human approval, and only an allowed
  call is forwarded. The request body limit is {guardrailNotes.bodyLimit}.
</p>
<p>
  The approval webhook signs requests and responses with HMAC-SHA256. Each approval is single-use,
  tied to the SHA-256 hash of the exact call, and times out to deny after
  {guardrailNotes.approvalTimeout}, so a captured approval cannot be replayed against a different
  amount.
</p>
<p>
  PII redaction covers emails, phones, SSNs, Luhn-checked card numbers, ABA routing numbers,
  labeled account numbers, and mod-97 IBANs. Each value becomes a stable typed placeholder, and the
  proxy restores the originals in the model's reply. The mapping is never sent out or logged. On the
  labeled corpus there were {corpus.false_positives} false positives on {corpus.must_survive} strings
  that must survive, {corpus.false_negatives} false negatives on {corpus.target_pii} target PII
  strings, and {corpus.known_misses} known misses: {guardrailNotes.knownMisses}.
</p>
<p>
  The audit log is append-only and hash-chained, signed with Ed25519. Each entry records a tool
  call (as a hash, never raw arguments), a rule decision, an approval, a redaction (counts only), or
  a forward. A signed tip file catches truncation, and a verify command points to the first tampered
  entry. The log uses in-process and file locks so more than one writer can append, and it can
  resume after a restart. The suite includes edit, delete, reorder, truncate, and wrong-key tamper
  cases.
</p>
<p>
  <code>make demo</code> runs every guardrail end to end: an allowed transfer, an approved and a
  denied $750 transfer, a rejected float amount, a PII round trip, and an audit verify that catches
  a one-byte tamper. The built demo binary ran end to end in {demoA} s and {demoB} s of real time,
  {guardrailNotes.demoCompile}. {guardrailNotes.demoNotABenchmark} GitHub Actions CI runs tests, vet,
  and the race detector.
</p>
<p class="note">
  Project notes. The $750 transfer is a step in the demo, not a second rule. The approval line in
  the notes is still $500. Demo times: {metrics.demo_runtime_seconds.note}.
  {guardrailNotes.demoNotABenchmark}
</p>

<h3 class="role-title">Milestones</h3>
{
  milestones.map((row) => (
    <p class="milestone">
      <Stamp status={row.kind} />
      {row.summary}{" "}
      {row.milestone.startsWith("5") ? `${row.test_cases} leaf tests` : `${row.test_cases} test cases`},{" "}
      {row.test_functions} test functions. <a href={row.pr}>PR #{row.prNumber}</a>
    </p>
  ))
}
<p class="note">
  guardrail-metrics.json. Counts are the suite size recorded at that milestone. Every milestone is
  shipped on its own pull request. {env.method}. Go {env.go} · {env.os_arch} · {env.cpu} ·
  GOMAXPROCS={env.gomaxprocs}.
</p>

<figure class="figure">
  <div aria-hidden="true" set:html={diagram} />
  <figcaption>Fig. 1 — Guardrail proxy, each branch in reading order. Every node is shipped.</figcaption>
</figure>

<figure class="figure">
  <div aria-hidden="true" set:html={checkChart} />
  <div class="sr-only">
  <table>
    <caption>Median check latency in nanoseconds per operation</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">{allow.path}</th><td>{formatInt(allow.ns_op)} ns/op</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">{needs.path}</th><td>{formatInt(needs.ns_op)} ns/op</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Fig. 2 — Median check latency, ns/op (lower is better)</figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="note">
  go test -bench · Go {env.go} · {env.os_arch} · {env.cpu} · GOMAXPROCS={env.gomaxprocs}. {env.method}.
  {guardrailNotes.checkAllocs} allocations and {guardrailNotes.checkBytes} bytes per check.
  {guardrailNotes.checksPerSecSentence} {guardrailNotes.checksCaveat}
</p>

<figure class="figure">
  <div aria-hidden="true" set:html={midChart} />
  <div class="sr-only">
  <table>
    <caption>Proxy, redaction, and audit append medians</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">{proxyAllow.path}</th><td>{formatInt(proxyAllow.ns_op)} ns/op</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">{redaction.path}</th><td>{formatInt(redaction.ns_op)} ns/op</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">{roundTrip.path}</th><td>{formatInt(roundTrip.ns_op)} ns/op</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">{append.path}</th><td>{formatInt(append.ns_op)} ns/op</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    Fig. 3 — Proxy, redaction, and audit append, median ns/op (lower is better). Audit verify is not
    on this chart.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="note">
  {metrics.notes} {guardrailNotes.approvalUsSentence} {guardrailNotes.approvalUsCaveat} Difference
  computed from the two proxy medians: {formatInt(approvalDiff)} ns. Redaction: {guardrailNotes.redactionAllocs}
  allocs/op on a 572-byte prompt. Append, with fsync of both the log and the tip file: {guardrailNotes.appendAllocs}
  allocs/op.
</p>
<p>
  Signing and checking one approval, with no network, takes a median of {formatInt(sign.ns_op)} ns.
  {guardrailNotes.approvalUsSentence} {guardrailNotes.approvalUsCaveat}
</p>
<p>
  {guardrailNotes.checksPerSecSentence} {guardrailNotes.checksCaveat} For the first milestone,
  {guardrailNotes.vetSentence}
</p>
<p>
  {guardrailNotes.verifySentence} The bench record for that verify is {formatInt(verify.ns_op)} ns.
  It is left off the chart because it is not on the same scale as the other medians.
</p>
<p class="note">
  Project notes for 421.05 ms, the allocation counts, the body limit, and the checks-per-second
  line. The nanosecond medians are the JSON values. {guardrailNotes.checksCaveat}
</p>
<pre><code>{latencyExcerpt}</code></pre>
<p class="note">Latency table, copied as recorded. The last line is the verify median, stated above and not plotted.</p>
